{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-register-vm.md | sed -n '60,115p'\"","aggregated_output":"    60\ttail flags that already make stack H differ.\n    61\t\n    62\t## 2. Compilation topology and stream contract\n    63\t\n    64\tThe general compilation topology is extended by chaining the register stage\n    65\tafter the stack projection:\n    66\t\n    67\t    Source -> Yang -> named AST datoms\n    68\t                         |\\\n    69\t                         | \\-> named semantic projection\n    70\t                         |\\\n    71\t                         +----> de Bruijn stack image (H)\n    72\t                                      |\n    73\t                                      +----> register image (R)\n    74\t\n    75\tThe register edge is not a fourth projection from raw AST datoms. It is a\n    76\tnew projection chained after the stack projection because its input is the\n    77\tvalidated stack image. The named stream, stack stream, and register stream\n    78\tcoexist and remain independently queryable or persistable.\n    79\t\n    80\tThe stage has the pure shape:\n    81\t\n    82\t    (compile-register stack-image-stream) -> register-image-stream\n    83\t\n    84\tEach input item contains the canonical stack vector, stack H, and descriptor\n    85\tmetadata. Each output contains the unchanged input reference plus a register\n    86\tdescriptor, register vector, source H, and R. The implementation may build\n    87\tindexes while evaluating the bounded item, but those indexes are local\n    88\tfunction data and are not written back into the stack image.\n    89\t\n    90\tThe stage refuses malformed stack images, unsupported opcodes, or an\n    91\tincomplete control-flow graph with qualified data diagnostics. It does not\n    92\tsilently fall back inside the compiler. A composition may route the original\n    93\tstack item to the stack VM after receiving that outcome.\n    94\t\n    95\t## 3. Register dimension\n    96\t\n    97\tThe descriptor declares:\n    98\t\n    99\t    +----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n   100\t    | Field                | Rule                                          |\n   101\t    +----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n   102\t    | Namespace            | :yin.debruijn.register/*                     |\n   103\t    | Contract version     | Explicit integer, included in descriptor H   |\n   104\t    | Source                | Validated stack H and canonical vector       |\n   105\t    | Register image       | Body ranges plus positional instructions     |\n   106\t    | Scalar encoding      | Reuse B1 scalar bytes, never projection NFC  |\n   107\t    | Lexical addressing   | :load-bound depth/position remains explicit  |\n   108\t    | Free addressing      | :load-free name remains exact                |\n   109\t    | Validation            | Shape, targets, body scope, register bounds  |\n   110\t    | Lift                 | Register image to stack semantics            |\n   111\t    +----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n   112\t\n   113\tThe register descriptor has its own validator because register operands and\n   114\tbody register counts are new facts. It reuses B1's public data rules and\n   115\tscalar encoder only through an explicit dependency; it does not call private\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
