{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-22 08:34:24 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-22 15:34:24 +07\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: 01a0bacb-3b91-7190-8412-3f1e85bb552a\n\nRecommendation: build it as a staged addition, with the register compiler and\nformat first, and the register VM kernel gated by benchmarks.\n\nI created [docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-register-vm.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-register-vm.md).\n\nThe design treats the register path as a pure, non-destructive stream\nprojection chained after the validated stack image:\n\n`named datoms -> de Bruijn stack image (H) -> register image (R)`\n\nThe stack image and H remain unchanged and independently addressable. The\nregister format has its own descriptor and R because its bytes, validator, and\nexecution model differ; R includes the source stack H, so it is a derived\nexecutable identity rather than a competing semantic identity.\n\nThe compiler uses deterministic CFG traversal, stack simulation, disjoint\nlocal and temporary register banks, canonical linear-scan allocation, stable\njoin moves, and cross-host golden fixtures. R1-R2 produce and validate code\nwithout requiring a register VM. R3 measures throughput, allocation, image\nsize, load time, and compile cost. R4 builds the kernel only if R3 shows a\nmaterial benefit. This directly addresses the historical risk of creating an\nunmeasured second evaluator.\n\nOwner decisions still needed:\n\n- Approve starting R1.\n- Define the material-benefit threshold for R3.\n- Decide whether to commission R4/R5 after the benchmark gate.\n- Set any target-specific register-file or spill constraints.\n- Approve register continuation lifting and cross-model park/resume semantics."}}
